# {{PROJECT_NAME}} Constitution

## Core principles

### Article I · Brainstorm before specifying

Every feature starts from a decided brainstorm record (`specs/NNN-slug/brainstorm.md`). The agent proposes at least five options, including doing nothing. The user chooses the direction. Rejected options become the spec's non-goals.
Rules: BRN-001, BRN-002, BRN-003, BRN-004.

### Article II · Specifications before code

No source code changes on a feature branch until the spec is approved by the user. Requirements are written in EARS form and every requirement is traced to a test. Plans follow design-doc structure with alternatives and cross-cutting concerns.
Rules: SDD-001 to SDD-006.

### Article III · Test first

Bug fixes start with a failing test. New behavior ships with tests in the same change. Changed lines reach 80% coverage.
Rules: TST-001 to TST-004, PR-005.

### Article IV · Small, reviewable changes

One task, one pull request, one concern. Commits and PR titles follow Conventional Commits. Agent-written changes need a human approval.
Rules: GIT-001 to GIT-005, PR-001 to PR-006, REV-001 to REV-004.

### Article IV-a · Trunk-based delivery

`main` is the only long-lived branch and is always releasable. Each task lives on its own short branch, unfinished features ship behind a flag, and `main` is protected on the server: pull request, human approval and passing checks required, no force pushes, no bypass.
Rules: BRA-001 to BRA-012.

### Article IV-b · Decisions are recorded

Architecturally significant decisions are recorded as ADRs in `docs/adr/` before or with the change they decide. Accepted ADRs are binding until superseded, and only a person accepts them.
Rules: ADR-001 to ADR-008.

### Article V · Consistent code

The formatter decides style. Names follow the language's Google style guide plus dev-standard naming rules. Compilers and type checkers run in strict mode.
Rules: NAM-001 to NAM-007, STY-001 to STY-005.

### Article VI · Secure by default

No secrets in the repo, validated input at every trust boundary, lockfiles committed, static analysis on every change.
Rules: SEC-001 to SEC-006, ERR-001, ERR-002.

{{#if react-web}}
### Article VII · One frontend stack

React with TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui and design tokens. Accessible to WCAG 2.2 AA and within Core Web Vitals budgets.
Rules: UI-001 to UI-010.
{{/if}}

{{#if deployed}}
### Article VIII · Production ready

Deployed services use the chosen OIDC provider, rate limit public endpoints, follow the API guidelines, ship security headers, define SLOs, and pass a production readiness review before first launch.
Rules: AUTH, ABU, API, WEB, REL, OBS, OPS, DAT groups in `.dev-standard/standards/production/`.
{{/if}}

